# ciphers.tflw — `T-1b` (tflw `D1372`): `probe ciphers` on the dogfood's own TLS sidecar, which
# offers only what nginx's defaults offer. `tflw-acceptance/security/ciphers.tflw` is the positive
# (8445, a listener that offers `NULL-SHA256`); this is the journey a team would actually write —
# *our* listener, nothing weak in its offer, and the test says so.
#
# `env secureLocal` grants the probe and names `tls-baseline.json`, which accepts the sidecar's one
# known serious finding — no HSTS header, on purpose, because the security corpus's positives are
# built on it. Accepted by fingerprint, so a *second* serious finding (a weak suite in the offer)
# still fails this line. Skipped under `env local`, which never reaches the sidecar.
#
#   npm run test:secure-local

@security @tls @identityOps @api
test "our TLS listener offers no weak suite" skip "only the TLS sidecar has an offer to probe, and env secureLocal addresses it" on env local
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  expect response has no serious security violations
